A||N||8||Monkeyman||wiring for aftermarker gauges||||5
Z||000000||Monkeyman||11-22-1999||08:49 PM||||I'm in the process of building a custom dash and am going to replace the stock gauge cluster with aftermarker gauges.  Only prob is that I don't have the slightest idea how to wire them up.  All I know is that I need electric gauges, right?  Anybody done this before?  How about some of you electronic wizard types (aka the sequential t.s. people!)?  Any help would be greatly appreciated!!!!||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||11-22-1999||10:26 PM||||I've had a little experience in swapping in aftermarket gauges. I put Auto Meter Sport electric speedo and tach in the Batmobile. I've also put in the ralley gauges. I've got some of the wire color code wrote down if you need it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/batdash2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||11-22-1999||11:56 PM||||I was going to say contact the BATBOY because I know he did some gauge mods. I have whatever wiring diagrams you might need that the BAT doesnt have.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||11-23-1999||12:22 AM||||I'll take all the wiring diagrams I can get.  I'm not keeping the stock gauge cluster at all.  My car doesn't have the GT gauges.  Do I still have the wiring for them tucked away somewhere?  Is it in with the wires that go to the standard cluster?  If I was really smart, I'd take this to a mechanic and let him wire everything up, but like I've said before, I'm real short on cash.  Besides, I know I can do this with a little time (plenty) and a little patience (almost none).  Next question...I know that I need an electric speedo.  What about the rest of the gauges?  Electric or mechanical?  Mechanical is definitely cheaper, but I have no intention of running new lines for everything.  Last question for a while...Do I need a new sending unit for the speedo?  I hate to be so long winded, but I have so many questions.  If I'm getting out of line, let me know.  I'll e-mail one of you.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||11-23-1999||11:35 AM||||The electric speedo I used worked just fine with the factory speed sensor. I'd double check with the company first before buying one though. I'd use all electric gauges, it'll work out better in the long run. The ralley gauges just splice into one of the existing instrument wiring harness connectors. I'll round up my wiring notes tonight and give you some of the codes here on the Forum. I think this project will be more expensive and time consuming than you may realize. I know it was for me. Remember, Cliff has a cool digital Fiero dash for sale.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||11-23-1999||02:21 PM||||I have <B>some</B> experience with aftermarket Fiero dashes...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Just ask me whatever you need to know.<P>BTW batboy, I just noticed the BatMan thingy hanging from the rearview mirror. That's funny!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||11-23-1999||06:16 PM||||batboy--I'd buy Cliffs' digital gauges in a heartbeat if I could afford them.  I can buy most of the gauges I need for about $350.  Seems to me that Cliffs' digital gauges are around a grand.  I just can't afford it.  I figure I'll spend around $550 on a custom dash including gauges, etc.  <P>Cliff--I'd be happy (real happy) to own one of your digital dashes.  If only you could come down on the price by about $650.:-)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cliff Pennock||11-23-1999||08:24 PM||||Well, many moons ago I was talking about a special introductory (is that a word?) discount price. The dash would be $500 for a period of time. I'm still planning on doing that, but it probably won't be before jan-feb of 2000. Right now I simply don't have the manpower for having those dashes made since we're currently doing some other projects that needs to get finished first... If you'd really want the FDD-X1 I suggest you wait a few months until we have the time to build them.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||11-23-1999||10:58 PM||||Ok, basically there are three main wiring harness connectors for the dash. The speedometer connector plugs in under the instrument cluster and to the left. In the back of the cluster is two connectors, on the left is C2 and on the right is C3. There are numbers or letters on the plastic plug for each wire. Lots of the wires I don't know, but it'll get you started. I'm sure Cliff can fill in the gaps or correct me if I'm wrong.<P>Speedo connector:<BR>B--white--jumper to D<BR>D--white--jumper to B<BR>P--black/white--ground<BR>R--purple/white--speedo<BR>S--yellow--speed sender<BR>T--yellow/black--12v switched<BR>U--brown--cruise<P>C2 (left) connector:<BR>4--black--ground<BR>7--gray--instrument panel lights<BR>9--pink/black--?<BR>10--dark green--?<BR>11--green--?<BR>12--gray--instrument panel lights<P>C3 (right) connector:<BR>2--two light blue wires--?<BR>3--gray--instrument panel lights<BR>4--two dark blue wires--?<BR>5--pink/black--12v switched<BR>6--brown--alternator charging light<BR>7--lavender--?<BR>8--yellow--?<BR>9--pink/black--?<BR>10--brown/white--?<BR>11--black--ground for instrument lights<BR>12--light green--?<BR>13--dark green--?<BR>14--tan/white--?<BR>15--gray--?<BR>17--tan--oil pressure sender<BR>18--white--tach||||reg||1
